{"id":"vasc-emerg","cards":[{"id":"vasc-ef813f20e","l":2,"f":"Most concerning symptom in acute limb ischaemia","b":"<b>paraesthesia</b> (nerve ischaemia = threatened limb)","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia"},{"id":"vasc-e52c8642d","l":2,"f":"Presentation of acute critical limb ischaemia among the options","b":"<b>paralysis</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia"},{"id":"vasc-e224afccd","l":2,"f":"Acute limb ischaemia","b":"<b>IV heparin + revascularization</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Limb Ischemia"},{"id":"vasc-e3df6c735","l":2,"f":"After revascularization of a traumatic ischaemic leg — the reperfusion complication","b":"<b>hyperkalaemia</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Limb Ischemia"},{"id":"vasc-e1e35e569","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Previous arterial disease?","b":"Usually absent","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e06d57d2c","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Previous arterial disease?","b":"Pre-existing <b>PAD / atherosclerosis</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e833268c5","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Previous ischemic symptoms?","b":"Usually absent","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e5076a39c","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Previous ischemic symptoms?","b":"Chronic history of pain / ischemia","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-ef94a5b0d","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Embolic source?","b":"Often present, e.g. <b>AF</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e264ac2d1","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Embolic source?","b":"Usually absent","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e79fedebd","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Onset?","b":"<b>Sudden, abrupt</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-ed0259ad7","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Onset?","b":"Acute worsening of chronic disease","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e4ea03541","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Severity?","b":"Usually <b>severe</b>, typical ischemia","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e33b7d471","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Severity?","b":"May be <b>less dramatic</b> (collaterals)","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-edb94ea86","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Collateral circulation?","b":"Usually absent / poor","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-ec6aa0c7c","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Collateral circulation?","b":"Often <b>well developed</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e8b4624c9","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Signs of chronic ischemia?","b":"Absent","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-edd12d84e","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Signs of chronic ischemia?","b":"Often present","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e83ec401a","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute embolism</b> — Arterial pathology?","b":"Abrupt occlusion of a <b>previously normal</b> artery","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e01d8b5e2","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic thrombosis</b> — Arterial pathology?","b":"Thrombosis over an <b>atherosclerotic</b> <b>stenosis</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e99e2a114","l":2,"f":"Chronic mesenteric ischaemia","b":"<b>postprandial pain and weight loss</b>","c":"Acute Limb Ischemia › Acute Embolism vs Acute-on-Chronic Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-ea1bac6b8","l":1,"f":"<b>Pulse finding</b> — list them","b":"<b>Femoral present, distal pulses absent</b> · <b>One femoral pulse absent</b> · <b>Both femoral pulses absent</b>","c":"Physical Examination › Clinical Assessment"},{"id":"vasc-ec3964a3f","l":1,"f":"<b>Likely level of occlusion</b> — list them","b":"Distal to the palpable femoral pulse, e.g. <b>femoropopliteal</b> segment · <b>Ipsilateral iliac</b> / proximal arterial occlusion · <b>Distal aorta / aortic bifurcation</b>","c":"Physical Examination › Clinical Assessment"},{"id":"vasc-e89db61fe","l":2,"f":"AF after an MI, sudden pale painful right leg, <b>femoral pulse present, absent below</b>, normal left leg","b":"<b>urgent embolectomy</b>","c":"Physical Examination › Clinical Assessment"},{"id":"vasc-e55603079","l":2,"f":"ICU patient in <b>septic shock</b> develops abdominal pain, distension and lactic acidosis","b":"<b>non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia</b>","c":"Site of occlusion"},{"id":"vasc-e9cbee101","l":2,"f":"Diabetic 72-year-old, <b>LLQ pain + bloody diarrhoea</b>, fever","b":"<b>ischaemic colitis</b>","c":"Site of occlusion"},{"id":"vasc-ed8b790cf","l":2,"f":"Most common cause of acute lower-limb ischaemia","b":"<b>embolization from the heart</b>","c":"Acute Arterial Thrombosis"},{"id":"vasc-e168afa55","l":2,"f":"80-year-old with <b>AF on digoxin</b>, 16 h of severe pain, distension, <b>guarding</b>, WBC 28, bowel-wall oedema","b":"<b>surgical exploration</b> (fixed — the source keys conflicted: antibiotics vs heparin)","c":"Severity of Ischemia"},{"id":"vasc-e0ee81a5e","l":1,"f":"<b>Classic acute embolic ALI</b> — First step?","b":"<b>IV heparin</b> (systemic anticoagulation)","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-ec2edf6f0","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic ischemia</b> — First step?","b":"<b>IV heparin</b>","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-e77f816d5","l":1,"f":"<b>Classic acute embolic ALI</b> — Imaging?","b":"<b>Do not delay</b> revascularization for routine imaging; examination and pulses usually localize the level. <b>CTA</b> only if time and limb viability allow (e.g. OR unavailable) and it won't delay treatment.","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-efe7b30c7","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic ischemia</b> — Imaging?","b":"<b>Usually needed</b>: disease may involve extensive atherosclerosis and <b>multiple stenotic segments</b>. Investigate to plan the intervention.","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-e942515b5","l":1,"f":"<b>Classic acute embolic ALI</b> — Revascularization?","b":"<b>Urgent</b> clot removal","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-e3a185518","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic ischemia</b> — Revascularization?","b":"<b>Angioplasty or bypass</b> rather than simple clot removal","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-e9b40511e","l":1,"f":"<b>Classic acute embolic ALI</b> — Time available?","b":"None: limb is threatened now","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-e420b6df9","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute-on-chronic ischemia</b> — Time available?","b":"Collaterals + viable limb may allow time; urgency depends on <b>ischemia severity</b>","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Investigation & Immediate Management"},{"id":"vasc-e86c4b0c2","l":1,"f":"<b>Open embolectomy / thrombectomy</b> — How?","b":"Arteriotomy; <b>Fogarty balloon</b> pulls the clot out","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e64432abc","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter-directed thrombolysis (endovascular)</b> — How?","b":"Catheter advanced <b>into the thrombus</b>; local <b>tPA</b>","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-ee57d5498","l":1,"f":"<b>Open embolectomy / thrombectomy</b> — Duration?","b":"Single operation","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e71113b71","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter-directed thrombolysis (endovascular)</b> — Duration?","b":"Infusion for <b>~12 h</b>, then reassessment / repeat angiography","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e3bab0427","l":1,"f":"<b>Open embolectomy / thrombectomy</b> — Typical use?","b":"Common treatment for <b>acute embolic</b> occlusion","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e06df3365","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter-directed thrombolysis (endovascular)</b> — Typical use?","b":"Selected patients: <b>unfit for open surgery</b>, <b>acute-on-</b> <b>chronic</b> disease","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e748593d6","l":1,"f":"<b>Open embolectomy / thrombectomy</b> — Why this route?","b":"Immediate clot extraction","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e62aa7131","l":1,"f":"<b>Catheter-directed thrombolysis (endovascular)</b> — Why this route?","b":"Local delivery avoids the large <b>systemic</b> dose a big thrombus would need → less <b>bleeding risk</b>","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-ef0eb6997","l":2,"f":"Gunshot to the arm — the finding suggesting arterial injury needing urgent angiography/surgery","b":"<b>diminished distal pulses</b>","c":"Systemic Anticoagulation › Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-ebeebe17e","l":1,"f":"<b>Potassium</b> — Effect on reperfusion?","b":"<b>Hyperkalemia</b>: can be rapid and fatal","c":"Ischemia Reperfusion Syndrome › Complications After Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e54ed5e44","l":1,"f":"<b>Potassium</b> — Prepare / treat?","b":"<b>Insulin + glucose</b>, <b>salbutamol (Ventolin)</b>, further urgent hyperkalemia management","c":"Ischemia Reperfusion Syndrome › Complications After Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e4a01d5a3","l":1,"f":"<b>Lactic acid</b> — Effect on reperfusion?","b":"<b>Metabolic acidosis</b>","c":"Ischemia Reperfusion Syndrome › Complications After Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-ed79fc34e","l":1,"f":"<b>Lactic acid</b> — Prepare / treat?","b":"<b>Sodium bicarbonate</b> for significant acidosis","c":"Ischemia Reperfusion Syndrome › Complications After Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e110a8fc2","l":1,"f":"<b>Other toxic breakdown products</b> — Effect on reperfusion?","b":"<b>Cardiac complications</b>, incl. potentially fatal <b>arrhythmias</b>","c":"Ischemia Reperfusion Syndrome › Complications After Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-e1eb0cddd","l":1,"f":"<b>Other toxic breakdown products</b> — Prepare / treat?","b":"Anticipate before flow is restored","c":"Ischemia Reperfusion Syndrome › Complications After Revascularization"},{"id":"vasc-ececf75b0","l":1,"f":"<b>True aneurysm</b> — What it is?","b":"Dilatation of the <b>native arterial wall</b>","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-effae428e","l":1,"f":"<b>False aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b> — What it is?","b":"<b>Contained hematoma</b> with persistent communication / flow from the artery","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-ed4a75d80","l":1,"f":"<b>True aneurysm</b> — Wall of the sac?","b":"The <b>arterial wall</b> itself","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e8e50e7c7","l":1,"f":"<b>False aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b> — Wall of the sac?","b":"<b>Fibrous tissue</b>, not normal arterial wall","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-ed5df36e9","l":1,"f":"<b>True aneurysm</b> — Shapes?","b":"<b>Fusiform</b>: circumferential <b>Saccular</b>: outpouching of part of the circumference","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e786c430f","l":1,"f":"<b>False aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b> — Shapes?","b":"Sac connected to the artery through a defect","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e74e64042","l":1,"f":"<b>True aneurysm</b> — Classic example?","b":"Degenerative AAA","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e69bf1246","l":1,"f":"<b>False aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b> — Classic example?","b":"Puncture site after <b>cardiac catheterization</b> fails to seal → continued flow into a contained hematoma","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e35bf29a4","l":1,"f":"<b>False aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm)</b> — Key distinction?","b":"If bleeding stops and <b>no communication persists</b> → it is a <b>hematoma</b>, not a pseudoaneurysm","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e28fd23df","l":2,"f":"<b>Local</b> complication of angiography/angioplasty","b":"<b>pseudoaneurysm</b> (at the puncture site)","c":"Types of Aneurysms › Definition & Classification"},{"id":"vasc-e1abf3fa2","l":2,"f":"AF + IHD with acute abdominal pain → most likely to reveal the diagnosis","b":"<b>CT scan abdomen</b> (CT angiography)","c":"AAA Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-e0ca4d231","l":1,"f":"<b>IVC / iliac veins</b> — Result?","b":"Venous obstruction → <b>DVT</b>","c":"AAA Presentation › 90–95%"},{"id":"vasc-eaf3224f3","l":1,"f":"<b>Bowel / duodenum</b> — Result?","b":"<b>Bowel obstruction</b>","c":"AAA Presentation › 90–95%"},{"id":"vasc-e4d1a4dbe","l":1,"f":"<b>Ureters</b> — Result?","b":"Obstruction → <b>hydronephrosis</b>","c":"AAA Presentation › 90–95%"},{"id":"vasc-e25fe0637","l":2,"f":"Elderly woman with <b>AF</b>, severe abdominal pain, <b>WBC 24, lactate 8</b>","b":"<b>bowel (mesenteric) ischaemia</b>","c":"AAA Presentation › 90–95%"},{"id":"vasc-e188db02a","l":1,"f":"<b>Open surgical repair</b> — Access?","b":"<b>Laparotomy</b>","c":"Treatment - Surgical › AAA Management"},{"id":"vasc-e6ddf76b2","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR)</b> — Access?","b":"<b>Femoral arteries</b>","c":"Treatment - Surgical › AAA Management"},{"id":"vasc-e2ec6f09a","l":1,"f":"<b>Open surgical repair</b> — Technique?","b":"Expose the aneurysm; insert a <b>vascular graft</b> with proximal and distal <b>anastomoses</b>","c":"Treatment - Surgical › AAA Management"},{"id":"vasc-eafd23e51","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR)</b> — Technique?","b":"Advance and deploy a <b>stent graft</b> inside the aorta to <b>exclude</b> the aneurysm","c":"Treatment - Surgical › AAA Management"},{"id":"vasc-e11e3d35a","l":1,"f":"<b>Open surgical repair</b> — Limitation?","b":"Traditional open operation with procedural risk","c":"Treatment - Surgical › AAA Management"},{"id":"vasc-e174b9a96","l":1,"f":"<b>Endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR)</b> — Limitation?","b":"Needs <b>suitable anatomy</b>: not every aneurysm is appropriate for EVAR","c":"Treatment - Surgical › AAA Management"},{"id":"vasc-e42355b97","l":2,"f":"<b>Pulsatile mass behind the knee</b> (popliteal aneurysm) — the most significant complication","b":"<b>thrombosis and distal embolization</b> (rupture is rare)","c":"Rupture AAA"},{"id":"vasc-e8dbe820c","l":2,"f":"SMA thrombosis → ischaemia of the <b>small intestine, caecum and ascending colon</b>; ischaemia of jejunum, ileum and caecum","b":"<b>SMA</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease"},{"id":"vasc-ec11ad523","l":1,"f":"<b>Carotid disease</b> — Arterial supply?","b":"Brain fed by <b>2 carotids + 2 vertebrals</b>; collaterals compensate, so even <b>significant stenosis may cause no</b> <b>symptoms</b> from low flow alone","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e3cb3d9a4","l":1,"f":"<b>Peripheral arterial disease</b> — Arterial supply?","b":"Collaterals develop around chronic stenoses","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-ee323e126","l":1,"f":"<b>Carotid disease</b> — Plaque?","b":"Superficial portion often <b>soft / vulnerable</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e49b8f893","l":1,"f":"<b>Peripheral arterial disease</b> — Plaque?","b":"Often <b>heavily calcified / hard</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e1e3bb790","l":1,"f":"<b>Carotid disease</b> — Typical plaque site?","b":"<b>Carotid bifurcation / proximal ICA</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e9294eca8","l":1,"f":"<b>Peripheral arterial disease</b> — Typical plaque site?","b":"Peripheral arteries","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e62ce0faa","l":1,"f":"<b>Carotid disease</b> — Main mechanism of symptoms?","b":"<b>Artery-to-artery microembolization</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e876d1cf2","l":1,"f":"<b>Peripheral arterial disease</b> — Main mechanism of symptoms?","b":"Reduced flow (ischemia)","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e65724329","l":1,"f":"<b>Severe stenosis (e.g. 90–99%)</b> — Flow over the plaque?","b":"<b>Still present</b> across an unstable plaque","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-ea321f88d","l":1,"f":"<b>Complete ICA occlusion</b> — Flow over the plaque?","b":"<b>None</b>: no residual lumen","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-ea704bd2c","l":1,"f":"<b>Severe stenosis (e.g. 90–99%)</b> — Embolic risk?","b":"<b>Continues</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e7b942ef6","l":1,"f":"<b>Complete ICA occlusion</b> — Embolic risk?","b":"Plaque material has nowhere to pass","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e5c8002b9","l":1,"f":"<b>Severe stenosis (e.g. 90–99%)</b> — Carotid endarterectomy?","b":"<b>May be warranted</b> if symptomatic","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-e074af288","l":1,"f":"<b>Complete ICA occlusion</b> — Carotid endarterectomy?","b":"<b>Generally not performed</b>","c":"Carotid Artery Disease › Definition & Pathophysiology"},{"id":"vasc-ebfbd10aa","l":1,"f":"<b>Motor / sensory (limbs)</b> — Side relative to the diseased carotid?","b":"<b>Contralateral</b>","c":"Risk Factors › Classification & Clinical Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-efbf2f9a5","l":1,"f":"<b>Motor / sensory (limbs)</b> — Example: left carotid disease?","b":"<b>Right</b>-sided limb weakness","c":"Risk Factors › Classification & Clinical Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-ee62c139f","l":1,"f":"<b>Amaurosis fugax (vision)</b> — Side relative to the diseased carotid?","b":"<b>Ipsilateral</b>","c":"Risk Factors › Classification & Clinical Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-e30c05d99","l":1,"f":"<b>Amaurosis fugax (vision)</b> — Example: left carotid disease?","b":"Transient <b>left</b>-eye visual loss","c":"Risk Factors › Classification & Clinical Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-efbcc3005","l":2,"f":"Transient right-hand weakness for 1 hour + <b>80% left ICA stenosis</b>","b":"presentation = <b>transient ischaemic attack</b>","c":"Risk Factors › Classification & Clinical Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-e9c1a4937","l":2,"f":"Stable penetrating thigh wound, <b>non-expanding haematoma, weak but palpable pulses</b> (soft signs)","b":"<b>CT angiography</b>","c":"Risk Factors › Classification & Clinical Presentation"},{"id":"vasc-e662af485","l":1,"f":"<b>Carotid duplex ultrasound</b> — Role?","b":"<b>Preferred</b> (exam- important)","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-e34a348d3","l":1,"f":"<b>Carotid duplex ultrasound</b> — Key points?","b":"<b>Duplex, not Doppler alone.</b> Measures velocity <b>before, within and</b> <b>after</b> the stenosis → hemodynamic estimate of severity. The <b>%</b> <b>stenosis determines management</b>.","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-e0409022d","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Role?","b":"Alternative / additional","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-e08a8795a","l":1,"f":"<b>CT angiography</b> — Key points?","b":"Shows the <b>anatomical narrowing</b> and vascular anatomy","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-efb64d493","l":1,"f":"<b>Angiography</b> — Role?","b":"Also evaluates disease","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-e06de3443","l":1,"f":"<b>Angiography</b> — Key points?","b":"Choice depends on <b>availability</b> and clinical circumstances","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-ed407f3b1","l":2,"f":"A carotid bruit is heard","b":"keyed <b>auscultate the precordium</b> (a radiating aortic-stenosis murmur can mimic a carotid bruit); a true bruit is then assessed with <b>carotid duplex</b>","c":"Risk Factors › Investigations"},{"id":"vasc-e7c49e82f","l":2,"f":"Hard sign of vascular injury","b":"<b>active pulsatile bleeding / expanding haematoma</b>","c":"Carotid Endarterectomy"},{"id":"vasc-e2fa18159","l":1,"f":"<b>Symptomatic (stroke, TIA, amaurosis fugax) + &gt;50% ipsilateral stenosis</b> — Management?","b":"Consider carotid intervention, particularly <b>carotid endarterectomy</b> <b>(CEA)</b>","c":"Carotid Endarterectomy › Treatment"},{"id":"vasc-e4b726f35","l":1,"f":"<b>Symptomatic + &lt;50% stenosis†</b> — Management?","b":"<b>Best medical therapy</b> (antiplatelet, statin, risk-factor control)<b>†</b>","c":"Carotid Endarterectomy › Treatment"},{"id":"vasc-e88178313","l":1,"f":"<b>Asymptomatic stenosis†</b> — Management?","b":"Best medical therapy; CEA only for <b>selected high-grade</b> stenosis (guidelines commonly use <b>60–70%</b>)<b>†</b>","c":"Carotid Endarterectomy › Treatment"},{"id":"vasc-e93513484","l":1,"f":"<b>Complete ICA occlusion</b> — Management?","b":"<b>No endarterectomy</b>: nothing left to embolize through","c":"Carotid Endarterectomy › Treatment"}]}